many people think the transactions in  bitcoin are anonymous  that's wrong  they're not  they're technically what's known as  pseudonymous  and if your ability to analyze the  blockchain which remember is public and  everybody can see  you have an ability to identify  transactions on bitcoin with a level of  detail and specificity that you can't do  with for example cash  if somebody pays cash for drugs  there's no public record of the fact  that the cash moved in one direction and  the heroin moved in another  intriguingly with bitcoin you can trace  these transactions if you're  sufficiently sophisticated  want evidence of that really easy have a  look at the indictment that mueller's  office issued against the 12 russian  hackers on july 13 2018.  if you read that indictment there's very  specific information there about how the  russians paid for servers and for other  aspects of their hacking activity with  bitcoin  now how is it that mueller's office  would know that certain servers and  other services were paid for with  bitcoin  simple answer  they're able to read the blockchain  they can follow the transactions from  one wallet which has a public number to  another wallet that has another public  number and using other sources they're  able to figure out who's behind the  wallet  and they're able to figure out the names  of the people behind the wallet  simple story  if the russian hackers can't hide their  identity from mueller's office  neither can you  so  bitcoin is not really anonymous  vis-a-vis the ability of sophisticated  parties to read and to analyze the  blockchain  now clearly some people don't like that  idea so they're trying to develop  technologies that are more secure than  bitcoin in terms of providing higher  levels of anonymity  they're services like monero they're  services like zcash but it remains to be  seen as to whether those systems can be  cracked and whether they really are  anonymous or just more difficult to  interpret than bitcoin  the jury's out on that
 

